export const F6={title:'Severe bleeding, wounds and shock (Skill IV)',hook:'A person with severe bleeding from an arm or leg can bleed to death in less than five minutes—often before an ambulance arrives. Firm, direct pressure and a properly placed tourniquet are skills anyone can learn, and they are the core of the national Stop the Bleed campaign. Skill IV tests both, along with care for shock.',objectives:['Recognize life-threatening bleeding and the three types of bleeding.','Control bleeding with direct pressure, dressings and a tourniquet following HOSA Skill IV.','Recognize and care for shock.','Care for minor wounds, impaled objects and amputations.'],sections:[
['Types of bleeding and why it is dangerous',[
'**Arterial bleeding** is bright red and **spurts** with each heartbeat; it is the most dangerous because it loses blood fast. **Venous bleeding** is darker red and **flows steadily**. **Capillary bleeding** **oozes** slowly from scrapes and usually clots on its own. **Life-threatening bleeding** is spurting or pulsing, flowing steadily, pooling on the ground, soaking through clothing or bandages, or bleeding from a missing limb.',
'An adult has about **5 liters** of blood. Losing a large fraction quickly causes **shock**, organ failure and death. External bleeding is visible, but **internal bleeding**—into the abdomen, chest or thigh after trauma—may show only as bruising, a swollen or rigid abdomen, coughing or vomiting blood and signs of shock. Suspect internal bleeding after car crashes, falls and blows to the trunk and call 911.',
sort('Type of bleeding',['Arterial','Venous','Capillary'],[['Bright red and spurting',0],['Dark red, steady flow',1],['Slow ooze from a scrape',2],['Pulses with the heartbeat',0],['Clots quickly on its own',2]])]],
['Direct pressure: Skill IV steps 1–3',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety, state ARC or AHA and put on **PPE**—gloves and eye protection. **2.** Respond to Rescuer 1’s call for help (Rescuer 2 performs this skill). Introduce yourself and get **consent**. **3.** Expose the wound and apply **firm, steady, constant direct pressure**, with **one hand stacked on top of the other**, over a sterile dressing or clean cloth. Push hard with locked arms and body weight—soft pressure does not stop arterial bleeding.',
'If blood soaks through, **do not remove the first dressing**; add more on top and keep pressing. Once bleeding is controlled, a **pressure bandage**—a roller bandage wrapped snugly over the dressing—holds pressure in place. Check **circulation beyond the bandage** (skin color, warmth, sensation, capillary refill); if fingers or toes become cold, pale or numb, loosen it slightly. For deep wounds of the neck, armpit or groin where a tourniquet cannot be used, trained rescuers **pack the wound** with gauze and hold pressure.',
order('Direct pressure',['Scene safety, ARC or AHA, PPE','Get consent','Expose the wound','Place a dressing over it','Press hard with stacked hands','Add dressings without removing the first','Apply a pressure bandage and check circulation'])]],
['Applying a tourniquet: Skill IV step 4',[
'If direct pressure does not control life-threatening bleeding from an **arm or leg**, or cannot be maintained, apply a **tourniquet**: **a.** place it **2–3 inches above the wound**, **not over a joint**; **b.** **pull the strap tight** and secure it; **c.** **twist the windlass** until the **bleeding stops**; **d.** **secure the windlass** in its clip; **e.** **note the time** it was applied (write it on the tourniquet or the patient). If one tourniquet does not stop the bleeding, a second may be placed above the first.',
'Tourniquets **hurt**—tell the patient so—and should **never be loosened** by a first aider once applied; only EMS or hospital staff remove them. Do not cover the tourniquet, so responders can see it. Modern commercial tourniquets (windlass type) are safe for several hours. Improvised tourniquets from narrow cords or wire can damage tissue and often fail.',
order('Tourniquet steps',['Place 2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint','Pull the strap tight','Twist the windlass until bleeding stops','Secure the windlass','Note the time','Leave it uncovered and do not loosen it'])]],
['Recognizing and treating shock: Skill IV steps 5–6',[
'**Shock** occurs when the body’s organs do not get enough oxygen-rich blood. Causes include severe bleeding (**hypovolemic**), heart failure (**cardiogenic**), severe allergic reaction (**anaphylactic**), severe infection (**septic**) and spinal cord injury (**neurogenic**). Signs include **restlessness or anxiety**, **pale, cool, moist skin**, **rapid, weak pulse**, **rapid breathing**, **thirst**, **nausea** and later **confusion** and **decreased responsiveness**. Blood pressure drops late.',
'**Skill IV:** **5.** Treat for shock: **a.** have the patient **lie on their back**; **b.** **cover the patient** to keep them warm. Do not give food or drink. Keep the patient calm and still, continue controlling bleeding and monitor breathing and responsiveness. **6.** Call EMS (name, location, phone, situation, number and condition of patients; hang up when told). **7.** Change gloves and assist Rescuer 1 with BLS. Raising the legs is no longer routinely taught; follow your organization’s guidance.',
sort('Sign of shock?',['Sign of shock','Not a typical sign'],[['Pale, cool, moist skin',0],['Rapid, weak pulse',0],['Restlessness and anxiety',0],['Slow, strong pulse with warm dry skin',1],['Thirst and nausea',0],['Rapid breathing',0]])]],
['Minor wounds, impaled objects and amputations',[
'For **minor wounds** (abrasions, small cuts), wash your hands, wear gloves, rinse the wound with clean running water, apply an antibiotic ointment if no allergies, and cover with a sterile bandage. Seek care for deep wounds, wounds that gape, animal or human bites, puncture wounds and signs of infection (redness, warmth, swelling, pus, fever). Check **tetanus** immunization status.',
'**Impaled objects:** **do not remove** them; stabilize the object with bulky dressings around it and control bleeding around it. **Amputations:** control bleeding (a tourniquet is often needed), then wrap the amputated part in sterile gauze, put it in a sealed plastic bag and place the bag on **ice and water**—never directly on ice—and send it with the patient. **Nosebleeds:** have the person sit, lean **forward** and pinch the soft part of the nose for about 10 minutes.',
match('Wound and care',[['Impaled object','Stabilize in place; do not remove'],['Amputated finger','Wrap, bag and place on ice water'],['Nosebleed','Lean forward and pinch the soft nose'],['Abrasion','Rinse with clean water and cover'],['Signs of infection','Redness, warmth, swelling, pus'],['Bleeding soaks through','Add dressings on top']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Glass injury',{a:{text:'A worker has bright red blood spurting from a deep cut on the forearm after a window broke. (Scenario invented for practice.) After PPE and consent, what is first?',choices:[['Firm direct pressure with stacked hands over a dressing','b','Correct. Pressure first.',true],['Wash the wound under the tap for 5 minutes','b','Control bleeding first.',false]]},b:{text:'Blood keeps spurting through the dressings despite hard pressure. What now?',choices:[['Apply a tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound, tighten until bleeding stops and note the time','c','Correct.',true],['Remove all dressings and start over','c','Do not remove dressings.',false]]},c:{text:'Bleeding stops. The worker is pale and anxious. You lay them on their back, cover them, call EMS and then help your partner.',end:true}})]],
["Bleeding control in the event and in the community",["In Skill IV, the judge watches that pressure is **firm, steady and constant**—not lifted to peek—and that the tourniquet is placed **2–3 inches above** the wound and **not over a joint**, tightened until bleeding stops and the **time is noted**. Tell the patient-actor what you are doing and why it will hurt. If the judge indicates the tourniquet has not stopped the bleeding, tighten further or place a second tourniquet above the first.","Public **bleeding control kits** are now placed beside AEDs in many schools, stadiums and airports. They contain a tourniquet, gauze for packing, gloves and instructions. The **Stop the Bleed** program teaches the simple sequence of calling 911, finding the source of bleeding and applying pressure, packing or a tourniquet.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Stop the Bleed sequence","steps":["Ensure your own safety","Call 911","Find the bleeding source","Apply firm pressure","Pack deep wounds or apply a tourniquet on a limb","Keep pressure until help arrives"]}]]
],takeaways:['Arterial bleeding spurts bright red, venous flows dark red, capillary oozes; life-threatening bleeding spurts, pools or soaks bandages.','Skill IV: PPE and consent, firm steady direct pressure with stacked hands; add dressings without removing; tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint, pull tight, twist the windlass until bleeding stops, secure and note the time.','Shock: pale, cool, moist skin, rapid weak pulse, anxiety, thirst; lay the patient on their back and cover them, nothing by mouth, call EMS; stabilize impaled objects; amputated parts in a bag on ice water.'],terms:[['Tourniquet','A band tightened above a limb wound to stop severe bleeding.'],['Shock','Inadequate blood flow and oxygen to the body’s organs.'],['Pressure bandage','A bandage wrapped snugly to hold pressure on a dressing.']],mcqs:[q('Which type of bleeding spurts bright red blood?','Arterial','Venous','Capillary','Internal only','It pulses with the heartbeat.'),q('Where is a tourniquet placed?','2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint','Directly on the wound','Below the wound','Over the elbow','Above the injury.'),q('If blood soaks through a dressing, you should:','Add more dressings on top and keep pressing','Remove it and replace it','Stop pressure','Pour water on it','Keep the clot.'),q('After applying a tourniquet, you should:','Note the time it was applied','Loosen it every 5 minutes','Cover it completely','Remove it when the patient complains','Only EMS removes it.'),q('Which sign suggests shock?','Pale, cool, moist skin','Warm, flushed skin','Slow, strong pulse','Calm behavior','Plus rapid, weak pulse.'),q('Per Skill IV, how is shock treated?','Lay the patient on their back and cover them','Give food and water','Have them walk','Sit them in a hot car','Keep warm.'),q('What should be done with an impaled object?','Stabilize it in place','Pull it out','Twist it','Push it deeper','Removal may worsen bleeding.'),q('How should an amputated part be transported?','Wrapped in gauze, sealed in a bag and placed on ice and water','Directly on ice','In warm water','Left at the scene','Never directly on ice.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to care for a patient with life-threatening bleeding from the leg following HOSA Skill IV, including when and how to apply a tourniquet and how to treat shock.',rubric:[['Preparation',['scene','ppe','gloves','consent','introduce']],['Direct pressure',['direct pressure','firm','steady','stacked','dressing','add','do not remove']],['Tourniquet',['2-3 inches','above','not','joint','pull','windlass','bleeding stops','secure','time']],['Shock and EMS',['shock','pale','cool','rapid','back','cover','ems','911','change gloves']]],model:'I make sure the scene is safe, state whether I follow ARC or AHA, put on gloves and eye protection, introduce myself and ask for consent. I expose the wound and apply firm, steady, constant direct pressure over a sterile dressing with one hand stacked on top of the other, using my body weight. If blood soaks through, I add more dressings on top rather than removing the first one. Because the bleeding is life-threatening and does not stop with direct pressure, I apply a tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound and not over the knee joint, pull the strap tight, twist the windlass until the bleeding stops, lock the windlass in place and write down the time it was applied. I tell the patient it will hurt, leave the tourniquet uncovered and never loosen it. I then watch for shock—restlessness, pale, cool, moist skin, a rapid, weak pulse, fast breathing and thirst—and treat it by having the patient lie on their back and covering them to keep them warm, giving nothing to eat or drink. I call EMS with my name, location, phone number, the situation and the number and condition of patients, staying on the line until told to hang up, then change gloves and help my partner with CPR.'},sources:[RULES,STOP_BLEED,MEDLINE_BLEED,MEDLINE_SHOCK]};

export const F7={title:'Fractures, splinting and head, neck and spine injuries (Skill VI)',hook:'Falls are the leading cause of injury in older adults, and sports injuries send millions of young people to emergency departments every year. A well-placed splint reduces pain, prevents further damage and can protect blood vessels and nerves. Skill VI tests the care of an open fracture—a broken bone that has torn through the skin.',objectives:['Recognize fractures, dislocations, sprains and strains.','Splint an open fracture following HOSA Skill VI.','Apply RICE for soft-tissue injuries.','Recognize and care for head, neck and spinal injuries.'],sections:[
['Musculoskeletal injuries',[
'A **fracture** is a break in a bone. In a **closed fracture** the skin is intact; in an **open fracture** the bone end breaks the skin or a wound extends to the bone, adding the risk of **infection** and **bleeding**. A **dislocation** moves a bone out of its normal position at a joint. A **sprain** stretches or tears **ligaments** (bone to bone), and a **strain** stretches or tears **muscles or tendons** (muscle to bone).',
'It is often impossible to tell these injuries apart without an X-ray, so **treat any painful, swollen, deformed injury as a fracture**. Signs listed on the HOSA rating sheet include **deformity**, **abnormal motion**, **pain**, **swelling** and **bone fragments**; others are bruising, inability to move or bear weight and a grating sound or feeling (crepitus). Call 911 for open fractures, injuries of the head, neck, back, hip or thigh, deformity with loss of circulation and multiple injuries.',
match('Injury and definition',[['Closed fracture','Broken bone with intact skin'],['Open fracture','Broken bone with a wound through the skin'],['Dislocation','Bone out of position at a joint'],['Sprain','Stretched or torn ligament'],['Strain','Stretched or torn muscle or tendon'],['Crepitus','Grating sound or feeling of broken bone ends']]),
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['bone','Bone','Breaks in a fracture; splint the joints above and below.'],['ligament','Ligament','Connects bone to bone; stretched or torn in a sprain.'],['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Cushions bone ends inside the joint.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Encloses the joint; torn in many dislocations.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Lines the capsule; swelling after injury.']])]],
['Caring for an open fracture: Skill VI steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety, state ARC or AHA, put on PPE, introduce yourself and get consent. **2.** Respond to Rescuer 1’s call. **3.** Look for **signs of a fracture**: deformity, motion, pain, swelling, bone fragments. **4.** **Check circulation and sensation beyond the injury**—skin color and temperature, capillary refill, pulse, and whether the patient can feel your touch and wiggle fingers or toes. **5.** Find **splint material** (commercial splint, rolled magazine, board or folded blanket) and **cover the broken skin with a clean or sterile cloth** to reduce infection risk and control bleeding without pressing on the bone end.',
'**Do not push** a protruding bone back in, and do not try to straighten an angled limb unless circulation is lost and you are trained to do so. Control bleeding with pressure **around** the bone, not on it. Remove rings, watches and bracelets on the injured limb before swelling makes removal impossible.',
order('Open fracture assessment',['Scene safety, ARC or AHA, PPE, consent','Look for deformity, motion, pain, swelling, bone fragments','Check circulation and sensation beyond the injury','Find splint material','Cover the broken skin with a clean or sterile cloth'])]],
['Splinting: Skill VI steps 6–10',[
'**6.** Splint the injury in the position found, **immobilizing the joints above and below** a broken bone (or the bones above and below an injured joint). **7.** Make sure the splint is **longer than the injury** area. **8.** **Pad** a hard splint for comfort and to fill gaps. **9.** **Secure the splint** with cravats or bandages, tying knots over the splint, not the injury. **10.** **Recheck circulation and sensation** beyond the injury; if fingers or toes are pale, cold, numb or tingling, loosen the ties. Then **keep the limb still**, apply ice wrapped in a cloth if available and call EMS.',
'Splint types include **rigid** (boards, commercial splints), **soft** (pillows, blankets, a sling and binder for the arm) and **anatomic** (taping an injured leg to the uninjured one, or a finger to the next finger—**buddy taping**). A **sling** supports an injured arm; a **binder** (cravat around the chest) holds it against the body.',
order('Splinting',['Splint in the position found','Immobilize the joints above and below','Use a splint longer than the injury','Pad the splint','Secure with cravats, knots over the splint','Recheck circulation and sensation; keep still'])]],
['Soft-tissue injuries and RICE',[
'For sprains, strains and bruises, use **RICE**: **Rest** the injured part; **Ice** it for about **20 minutes** at a time with a cloth barrier (never ice directly on skin); **Compression** with an elastic bandage if trained, checking circulation; and **Elevation** above heart level if it does not cause pain. Many sources now teach that rest and cold are most important and that gentle movement should return as pain allows.',
'Seek medical care if the person cannot bear weight, the joint is deformed, pain is severe or numbness occurs. Over-the-counter pain relievers may be used as directed for minor injuries if the person has no contraindications.',
flip('RICE',[['R','Rest the injured part'],['I','Ice about 20 minutes with a cloth barrier'],['C','Compression, checking circulation'],['E','Elevate if it does not cause pain'],['Never','Ice directly on skin'],['Seek care','Cannot bear weight or deformity']])]],
['Head, neck and spinal injuries',[
'Suspect a **head, neck or spinal injury** after falls from a height, diving into shallow water, car or motorcycle crashes, blows to the head and any unresponsive injured person. Signs include headache, confusion, unequal pupils, vomiting, seizures, blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose, bruising behind the ears or around the eyes, and—for the spine—neck or back pain, tingling, weakness or loss of movement in the limbs.',
'**Care:** call 911, keep the person still and **support the head in the position found** (manual stabilization), and monitor breathing. Do not move the person unless necessary for safety or CPR. A **concussion** is a mild traumatic brain injury; athletes with suspected concussion should be removed from play—“**when in doubt, sit them out**”—and evaluated. Worsening headache, repeated vomiting, confusion or drowsiness need emergency care.',
kase('Soccer collision',{a:{text:'A player collides with another and has a deformed lower leg with bone visible through the skin. (Scenario invented for practice.) After PPE and consent, what is next?',choices:[['Check circulation and sensation below the injury and cover the wound with a sterile cloth','b','Correct.',true],['Push the bone back in','b','Never push bone back.',false]]},b:{text:'You find a padded board splint. How should it be applied?',choices:[['Longer than the injury, immobilizing the knee and ankle, tied over the splint','c','Correct. Then recheck circulation.',true],['Short splint tied directly over the wound','c','Splint above and below.',false]]},c:{text:'Toes are warm and pink after splinting. You keep the leg still and call EMS.',end:true}})]],
["Splinting specific injuries",["An injured **arm** can be supported with a **sling** that holds the forearm across the chest and a **binder** that holds the arm to the body. For a **collarbone** injury, a sling and binder are usually enough. A broken **finger** can be **buddy-taped** to the next finger with padding between them. A lower-leg injury can be splinted with a padded board or an **anatomic splint**, securing the injured leg to the uninjured one with cravats.","Suspected injuries to the **hip, pelvis or thigh (femur)** can involve major blood loss; do not move the patient, keep them still and warm, watch for shock and call 911. Always splint only if it will not cause more pain or if the patient must be moved—if EMS will arrive soon and the patient is not moving, supporting the limb in the position found may be enough.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Injury and support","pairs":[["Forearm fracture","Rigid splint, then sling and binder"],["Collarbone injury","Sling and binder"],["Broken finger","Buddy tape with padding"],["Lower leg fracture","Padded board or anatomic splint"],["Suspected thigh fracture","Keep still, treat shock, call 911"],["Ankle sprain","RICE"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Open fracture breaks the skin; treat any painful, swollen, deformed injury as a fracture; signs: deformity, motion, pain, swelling, bone fragments.','Skill VI: check circulation and sensation beyond the injury, cover broken skin with a sterile cloth, splint in the position found, immobilize the joints above and below, splint longer than the injury, pad, secure, recheck and keep still.','RICE for sprains and strains (ice 20 minutes with a barrier); suspect spinal injury after falls and diving—support the head in the position found and call 911; when in doubt, sit them out.'],terms:[['Open fracture','A broken bone with a wound through the skin.'],['Splint','A device that immobilizes an injured body part.'],['Sprain','A stretched or torn ligament.']],mcqs:[q('What is an open fracture?','A broken bone with a wound through the skin','A broken bone with intact skin','A torn ligament','A torn muscle','Higher infection risk.'),q('A splint should immobilize:','The joints above and below the broken bone','Only the break','The whole body','Nothing','Prevents movement.'),q('What should be checked before and after splinting?','Circulation and sensation beyond the injury','Blood glucose','Pupils only','Temperature of the room','Fingers or toes.'),q('What covers the broken skin of an open fracture?','A clean or sterile cloth','Nothing','Ice directly','Tape only','Reduces infection.'),q('A sprain is an injury to a:','Ligament','Bone only','Tendon only','Nerve','Strain affects muscles or tendons.'),q('How long is ice applied at a time?','About 20 minutes','2 hours','1 minute','Continuously overnight','With a cloth barrier.'),q('How is a suspected neck injury cared for?','Support the head in the position found and call 911','Sit the person up','Have them walk it off','Turn the head side to side','Manual stabilization.'),q('A protruding bone in an open fracture should be:','Left in place and covered','Pushed back in','Pulled straight','Washed with soap','Do not push bone back.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to care for an open fracture of the lower leg following HOSA Skill VI, including assessment, wound care, splinting and reassessment.',rubric:[['Preparation and signs',['scene','ppe','consent','deformity','motion','pain','swelling','bone fragments']],['Circulation and wound',['circulation','sensation','beyond','sterile','clean cloth','cover','do not push']],['Splinting',['position found','joints above and below','longer','pad','secure','cravat']],['Reassessment and EMS',['recheck','circulation','keep still','ice','ems','911']]],model:'I make sure the scene is safe, state ARC or AHA, put on gloves and eye protection, introduce myself and get consent. I look for signs of a fracture—deformity, abnormal motion, pain, swelling and bone fragments—and because bone is visible through the skin, I treat it as an open fracture. I check circulation and sensation beyond the injury by checking the color and warmth of the toes, capillary refill and the foot pulse and asking whether the patient can feel my touch and wiggle the toes. I find splint material and cover the broken skin with a clean or sterile cloth, controlling bleeding with pressure around the bone rather than on it, and I never push the bone back in. I splint the leg in the position found, using a splint longer than the injury that immobilizes the joints above and below the break—the knee and the ankle. I pad the hard splint to fill gaps and secure it with cravats, tying the knots over the splint rather than the injury. Afterward I recheck circulation and sensation in the toes and loosen the ties if they are pale, cold or numb. I keep the limb still, apply a cold pack wrapped in cloth if available, watch for shock and call EMS with my name, location, phone number and the situation before helping my partner with BLS.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_FRACT,MEDLINE_HEAD,ARC_STEPS]};

export const F8={title:'Burns and electrical and chemical injuries (Skill VII)',hook:'Burns injure hundreds of thousands of people in the United States each year, many of them young children scalded by hot liquids. The right first aid in the first minutes—cooling the burn and protecting it—reduces pain, depth and scarring. Skill VII tests care for a severe burn and the shock that can follow.',objectives:['Classify burns by depth and estimate their extent.','Care for a severe burn following HOSA Skill VII.','Recognize burns that need emergency care.','Care for chemical, electrical and inhalation injuries.'],sections:[
['Classifying burns',[
'**Superficial (first-degree)** burns affect only the **epidermis**: red, dry, painful skin, like sunburn, healing in about a week. **Partial-thickness (second-degree)** burns reach into the **dermis**: red, **blistered**, moist, very painful skin. **Full-thickness (third-degree)** burns destroy all skin layers and may reach fat, muscle and bone: skin may be **white, leathery, brown or charred**, and it may be painless because nerves are destroyed, though surrounding areas hurt.',
'Burn severity depends on **depth**, **size**, **location** and the patient’s **age and health**. Size is estimated by the **rule of nines** (adult: each arm 9%, each leg 18%, front and back of the trunk 18% each, head 9%, genitals 1%) or the **rule of palms**—the patient’s palm with fingers is about **1%** of body surface. Infants and older adults have more serious outcomes from the same burn.',
{t:'w',kind:'burns'},
label('skin','Skin layers and burn depth',[['epidermis','Epidermis','Superficial burns stay in this outer layer.'],['dermis','Dermis','Partial-thickness burns reach it and form blisters.'],['hypodermis','Subcutaneous layer','Full-thickness burns can reach fat and below.'],['burn1','Superficial burn','Red, dry and painful.'],['burn2','Partial-thickness burn','Blistered, moist and very painful.'],['burn3','Full-thickness burn','White, leathery or charred; may be painless.']])]],
['Caring for a severe burn: Skill VII',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety—**stop the burning** (remove the person from the heat source; “stop, drop and roll” for burning clothing)—state ARC or AHA, put on PPE, introduce yourself and get consent. **2.** Respond to Rescuer 1’s call. **3.** **Cool the burn with cool running water for at least 10 minutes**; if running water is not available, use a **cool, wet compress**. Do not use ice, which damages tissue, and avoid cooling large burns so long that the person becomes **hypothermic**.',
'**4.** **Loosely cover the burn** with a **sterile, non-stick gauze** dressing or clean cloth to protect it and reduce pain from air. Do not break blisters, apply butter, ointments or creams to a severe burn, or remove clothing stuck to the burn. Remove rings and tight items before swelling. **5.** Treat for **shock**: have the patient **lie on their back** and **cover with a dry blanket** to keep warm. **6.** Call EMS, **7.** change gloves and help Rescuer 1 with BLS.',
order('Severe burn care',['Stop the burning; scene safety; PPE; consent','Cool with cool running water at least 10 minutes','Loosely cover with sterile non-stick gauze','Do not break blisters or apply butter','Treat shock: on the back, dry blanket','Call EMS'])]],
['When a burn needs emergency care',[
'Call 911 for burns that cause **trouble breathing**; cover **more than one body part** or a large area; involve the **head, neck, hands, feet or genitals**; are **full-thickness**; are caused by **chemicals, electricity or explosions**; or occur in **young children or older adults**. Also call for burns that **circle** a limb or the chest, which can cut off circulation or breathing as the tissue swells.',
'Minor superficial burns can be cooled, protected with a moisturizer or aloe and covered. Prevent scalds by setting water heaters to **120 °F (49 °C)** or lower, turning pot handles inward, keeping hot drinks away from children and testing bath water. Working **smoke alarms** on every level of the home halve the risk of dying in a house fire.',
sort('Call 911?',['Call 911','Minor burn first aid'],[['Small sunburn on the shoulder',1],['Burn on the face with singed nose hairs',0],['Electrical burn from an outlet',0],['Burn covering both legs',0],['Small superficial burn on a forearm from a pan',1],['Partial-thickness burn on an infant’s hand',0]])]],
['Chemical, electrical and inhalation injuries',[
'**Chemical burns:** protect yourself, **brush off dry chemicals**, then **flush with large amounts of cool running water for at least 20 minutes**, removing contaminated clothing and jewelry. For chemicals in the **eye**, flush from the nose side outward so the chemical does not enter the other eye. Call **Poison Help (1-800-222-1222)** or 911.',
'**Electrical burns:** **turn off the power** before touching the person; there may be small entry and exit wounds with severe internal damage and dangerous heart rhythms—be ready for CPR and an AED, and call 911. **Lightning** strike victims do not hold a charge and can be touched; begin CPR if needed. **Inhalation injury** from smoke or heat causes hoarseness, soot around the nose and mouth, singed facial hair and coughing; move the person to fresh air and call 911.',
match('Injury and first action',[['Dry chemical on the skin','Brush off, then flush with water'],['Liquid chemical in the eye','Flush 20 minutes from the nose outward'],['Electrical injury','Turn off the power first'],['Lightning strike','Safe to touch; start CPR if needed'],['Smoke inhalation','Move to fresh air and call 911'],['Burning clothing','Stop, drop and roll']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Kitchen fire',{a:{text:'A cook’s forearm and hand were burned by hot oil; the skin is red with large blisters. (Scenario invented for practice.) After stopping the burning and PPE, what next?',choices:[['Cool under cool running water for at least 10 minutes','b','Correct.',true],['Apply butter and pop the blisters','b','Never butter or break blisters.',false]]},b:{text:'The burn is cooled. How should it be covered?',choices:[['Loosely with sterile non-stick gauze','c','Correct.',true],['Tightly with adhesive tape directly on the burn','c','Loose and non-stick.',false]]},c:{text:'The cook is pale and anxious. You lay them on their back, cover them with a dry blanket and call EMS—hand burns need medical care.',end:true}})]],
["Burns in children and older adults",["Children have **thinner skin** and a **larger body surface area for their weight**, so the same hot liquid causes deeper burns and they lose fluid and heat faster. In infants, the **head** makes up a larger percentage of the body than in adults, so the pediatric rule of nines assigns more to the head and less to the legs. Older adults also have thin skin, heal more slowly and often have other illnesses, so even moderate burns can be serious.","Most childhood burns are **scalds** from hot drinks, bath water and cooking. Burns in an unusual pattern—such as sharply outlined “glove” or “stocking” burns or round cigarette burns—may signal **abuse** and must be reported. When cooling burns in small children, use cool water for 10 minutes but watch closely for **hypothermia**, then keep them warm with a dry blanket.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Higher-risk burn patient?","buckets":["Higher risk","Lower risk"],"items":[["A 9-month-old scalded by tea",0],["A healthy adult with a small sunburn",1],["An 80-year-old with a partial-thickness burn on the leg",0],["A teen with a tiny superficial burn on one finger",1],["A toddler with a burn on the hands",0],["A person with diabetes and a foot burn",0]]}]],
["Burn mistakes and myths",["Many home remedies make burns worse. **Butter, oil and toothpaste** trap heat and raise infection risk. **Ice** narrows blood vessels and can freeze already injured tissue. **Breaking blisters** removes a natural barrier against infection. Fluffy cotton sticks to the wound, so use non-stick dressings. Removing clothing stuck to a burn tears the skin—cut around it instead and leave the stuck part.","In the event, the judge looks for **cooling for at least 10 minutes**—not a brief rinse—and a dressing that is **loosely** applied. Verbalize that you would remove jewelry and watch for shock. If running water is not available in the scenario, say that you are using a cool compress and that you will keep it cool by rewetting it.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Severe burn: what the judge sees","steps":["States stop the burning and scene safety","Cools at least 10 minutes","Removes jewelry before swelling","Covers loosely with sterile non-stick gauze","Treats shock with a dry blanket","Calls EMS and changes gloves"]}]]
],takeaways:['Superficial: red, dry, painful; partial-thickness: blisters, very painful; full-thickness: white, leathery or charred, may be painless; estimate size with the rule of nines or palms (1%).','Skill VII: stop the burning, cool with running water at least 10 minutes (cool compress if none), loosely cover with sterile non-stick gauze, no ice, butter or popping blisters, treat shock (on back, dry blanket), call EMS.','Call 911 for breathing trouble, large burns, face, hands, feet or genitals, chemical, electrical or explosion burns and burns in young children or older adults; flush chemicals 20 minutes; turn off power before touching.'],terms:[['Partial-thickness burn','A burn into the dermis with blisters.'],['Full-thickness burn','A burn through all skin layers.'],['Rule of nines','A method for estimating the percentage of body surface burned.']],mcqs:[q('Pat has just finished cooling a superficial burn. What should Pat do next?','Cover the burn with a dry, nonstick, sterile dressing.','Apply a moisturizer.','Apply an antibiotic ointment.','Apply hydrocortisone cream.','HOSA sample question.'),q('How long should a burn be cooled with running water?','At least 10 minutes','10 seconds','1 minute','2 hours','Cool compress if no water.'),q('Which describes a partial-thickness burn?','Red, blistered and very painful','Red and dry only','White and leathery','Charred and painless','Second-degree.'),q('Per the rule of palms, the patient’s palm is about:','1% of body surface','9%','18%','25%','Rule of palms.'),q('What should be done first for an electrical injury?','Turn off the power','Touch the patient immediately','Pour water on them','Apply ice','Protect yourself.'),q('How long should a chemical burn be flushed?','At least 20 minutes','30 seconds','5 minutes','It should not be flushed','Brush off dry chemicals first.'),q('Which burn needs a 911 call?','A partial-thickness burn on the hands','A small sunburn','A tiny superficial burn','None','Hands are critical areas.'),q('Per Skill VII, how is shock treated in a burn patient?','On the back, covered with a dry blanket','Sitting in cold water for an hour','Standing up','Given hot coffee','Keep warm.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to care for a large partial-thickness burn following HOSA Skill VII, and explain how care differs for a chemical burn and an electrical burn.',rubric:[['Safety',['stop the burning','scene','ppe','consent','power']],['Cooling and covering',['cool','running water','10 minutes','compress','loosely','sterile','non-stick','no ice','blisters']],['Shock and EMS',['shock','back','blanket','warm','ems','911']],['Special burns',['chemical','brush','flush','20 minutes','electrical','turn off','cpr','aed','entry','exit']]],model:'First I make sure the scene is safe and stop the burning by removing the person from the heat source, then state ARC or AHA, put on PPE, introduce myself and get consent. I cool the burn with cool running water for at least 10 minutes, or with a cool, wet compress if running water is not available, avoiding ice and watching that the person does not get chilled. I then loosely cover the burn with a sterile, non-stick gauze dressing, without breaking blisters, applying butter or creams or pulling off clothing stuck to the skin, and I remove rings before swelling. Because large burns cause fluid loss, I treat for shock by having the patient lie on their back and covering them with a dry blanket. I call EMS with my name, location, phone number and the situation and stay on the line, then change gloves and help my partner. A chemical burn is different: I protect myself, brush off any dry chemical and then flush with large amounts of running water for at least 20 minutes while removing contaminated clothing, and I call Poison Help or 911. For an electrical burn, I make sure the power is turned off before touching the person, look for entry and exit wounds, call 911 and am ready to start CPR and use an AED because electricity can cause cardiac arrest.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_BURN,ARC_STEPS,POISON]};

export const F9={title:'Heat, cold and other environmental emergencies (Skill VIII)',hook:'Extreme heat kills more people in the United States than any other weather hazard, and heat stroke is a leading cause of death in young athletes. Cold, lightning, bites, stings and water are other environmental dangers. Skill VIII tests the care of heat exhaustion or heat stroke—and the difference between them can be life or death.',objectives:['Distinguish heat cramps, heat exhaustion and heat stroke.','Care for heat-related emergencies following HOSA Skill VIII.','Recognize and care for hypothermia and frostbite.','Give first aid for bites, stings, lightning and drowning.'],sections:[
['Heat-related illnesses',[
'**Heat cramps** are painful muscle spasms, usually in the legs or abdomen, during heavy exercise in heat. **Heat exhaustion** is caused by fluid and salt loss: **heavy sweating**, **cool, pale, clammy skin**, **headache**, **nausea or vomiting**, **dizziness**, weakness, fatigue and a fast, weak pulse; the body temperature is normal or slightly raised. **Heat stroke** is a life-threatening failure of the body’s cooling system: **very high body temperature** (often 104 °F/40 °C or higher), **hot skin that may be red and dry or moist**, **confusion**, **slurred speech**, **seizures** and **loss of consciousness**.',
'**Changes in mental status** are the key sign separating heat stroke from heat exhaustion. Risk factors include high heat and humidity, strenuous exercise, heavy clothing or equipment, dehydration, obesity, certain medications and the very young or old. Never leave children or pets in parked cars—inside temperatures rise quickly.',
sort('Heat exhaustion or heat stroke?',['Heat exhaustion','Heat stroke'],[['Heavy sweating with cool, clammy skin',0],['Confusion and slurred speech',1],['Body temperature 105 °F',1],['Headache, nausea and dizziness with normal thinking',0],['Seizure',1],['Fatigue and weakness after a hot practice',0]])]],
['Caring for heat-related emergencies: Skill VIII',[
'**Heat exhaustion:** move the patient to a **cool area**; have them **lie on their back with legs elevated about 12 inches**; **remove or loosen tight clothing**; apply **cool, damp cloths**; and if the patient is **conscious and able to swallow**, give **small sips of cool water or a sports drink—about 4 ounces every 15 minutes**. Stop fluids and call 911 if the patient vomits, refuses to drink or gets worse.',
'**Heat stroke:** call 911 and **cool the patient rapidly**. Move them to a cool area, **remove clothing**, and **immerse them in cool water up to the neck** (cold-water immersion is the best method). If immersion is not possible, **sponge with cool water** and place **ice packs at the wrists, ankles, armpits (axillae) and groin**, fanning to increase evaporation. **Watch for shock** and be ready to give CPR. Do not give fluids to a person who is confused or unresponsive.',
{t:'table',caption:'Skill VIII care',head:['Step','Heat exhaustion','Heat stroke'],rows:[['Location','Cool area','Cool area'],['Position','On back, legs up 12 inches','As needed; watch airway'],['Clothing','Remove or loosen','Remove'],['Cooling','Cool, damp cloths','Immerse to the neck, or sponge and ice packs at wrists, ankles, armpits, groin'],['Fluids','Sips, about 4 oz every 15 minutes if conscious','None if confused'],['911','If worsening or vomiting','Always']]}]],
['Cold emergencies',[
'**Hypothermia** occurs when body temperature falls below **95 °F (35 °C)**. Signs progress from **shivering**, numbness and clumsiness to **confusion**, slurred speech, drowsiness, **slow breathing and pulse** and eventually unconsciousness—shivering may **stop** in severe hypothermia. Care: call 911, move the person to a warm place, remove wet clothing, warm the **core** gradually with blankets, dry layers and skin-to-skin contact or warm (not hot) packs on the trunk, and give warm, non-alcoholic drinks only if fully alert. Handle gently and be ready for CPR.',
'**Frostbite** is freezing of tissue, usually the fingers, toes, ears and nose: skin is **numb**, white, grayish-yellow, waxy or hard. Get the person to a warm place and **do not rub** the area. Rewarm in **warm (not hot) water** (about 99–104 °F) for 20–30 minutes **only if there is no chance of refreezing**, loosely bandage with dry, sterile gauze between fingers and toes, do not break blisters and seek medical care.',
match('Cold emergency and care',[['Mild hypothermia with shivering','Warm place, dry clothing, blankets'],['Severe hypothermia','Handle gently, call 911, be ready for CPR'],['Frostbitten fingers','Warm water rewarming if no refreezing'],['Rubbing frostbite','Do not—it damages tissue'],['Wet clothing','Remove and replace with dry layers'],['Warm drinks','Only if fully alert']])]],
['Bites, stings, lightning and drowning',[
'**Insect stings:** scrape away a stinger with a flat edge, wash, apply a cold pack and watch for **anaphylaxis**. **Ticks:** grasp close to the skin with fine-tipped tweezers and pull steadily upward; wash the area. **Animal bites:** wash with soap and water, control bleeding, cover and seek care (rabies risk). **Snakebites:** call 911, keep the person calm and still with the bitten limb at heart level and remove tight items; do **not** cut, suck, ice or apply a tourniquet.',
'**Lightning:** “When thunder roars, go indoors.” Lightning victims can be touched safely; check breathing and give CPR and AED as needed. **Drowning:** reach or throw—don’t go—unless trained; once the person is out of the water, check breathing. Because drowning is a breathing emergency, **rescue breaths** are especially important. Prevent drowning with supervision, swimming lessons, life jackets and pool fencing.',
order('Removing a tick',['Use fine-tipped tweezers','Grasp the tick close to the skin','Pull upward with steady, even pressure','Do not twist or crush','Clean the area with soap and water or alcohol','Watch for rash or fever'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Summer practice',{a:{text:'During a hot football practice, a player is confused, has a temperature of 105 °F and hot skin. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is this?',choices:[['Heat stroke—call 911 and cool rapidly','b','Correct. Mental status change is key.',true],['Heat cramps—stretch the legs','b','Confusion means heat stroke.',false]]},b:{text:'A cold-water tub is available. What do you do?',choices:[['Remove equipment and clothing and immerse the player up to the neck','c','Correct. Fastest cooling.',true],['Give a large bottle of water to drink','c','Do not give fluids to a confused person.',false]]},c:{text:'You monitor breathing and watch for shock until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Preventing environmental emergencies",["**Heat:** acclimatize gradually over about 1–2 weeks, schedule practices in cooler hours, drink fluids before, during and after activity, wear light, loose clothing and take rest breaks in the shade. Coaches should have a **cold-water immersion tub** available and an emergency action plan. Check on older neighbors during heat waves and never leave anyone in a parked car.","**Cold:** dress in layers, cover the head, hands and feet, stay dry and avoid alcohol, which increases heat loss. **Water:** swim with a buddy, use life jackets and fence pools. **Lightning:** go indoors or into a hard-topped vehicle when thunder is heard and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder. **Bites and stings:** use insect repellent, check for ticks after outdoor activity and people with severe allergies should carry two epinephrine auto-injectors.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Hazard and prevention","pairs":[["Heat illness","Acclimatize, hydrate, rest in shade"],["Hypothermia","Layers, stay dry, avoid alcohol"],["Drowning","Supervision, life jackets, pool fencing"],["Lightning","Go indoors when thunder roars"],["Tick-borne disease","Repellent and tick checks"],["Severe sting allergy","Carry two auto-injectors"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cool clammy skin, headache, nausea, normal thinking; heat stroke: very high temperature, hot skin, confusion, seizures—mental status change is the key sign.','Skill VIII: heat exhaustion—cool area, on back with legs up 12 inches, loosen clothing, cool damp cloths, sips about 4 oz every 15 minutes if conscious; heat stroke—call 911, remove clothing, immerse to the neck or sponge with ice packs at wrists, ankles, armpits and groin, watch for shock.','Hypothermia below 95 °F: warm the core gently; frostbite: do not rub, rewarm in warm water only if no refreezing; snakebite: keep still at heart level, no cutting or ice; lightning victims are safe to touch.'],terms:[['Heat stroke','Life-threatening overheating with altered mental status.'],['Hypothermia','Body temperature below 95 °F (35 °C).'],['Frostbite','Freezing of body tissue.']],mcqs:[q('Which sign best separates heat stroke from heat exhaustion?','Confusion or altered mental status','Sweating','Thirst','Muscle cramps','Heat stroke affects the brain.'),q('How should a patient with heat exhaustion be positioned per Skill VIII?','On the back with legs raised about 12 inches','Sitting in the sun','Standing','Prone','Cool area.'),q('How much fluid is given to a conscious heat exhaustion patient?','Small sips, about 4 ounces every 15 minutes','A gallon at once','None','Hot coffee','If able to swallow.'),q('What is the best way to cool a heat stroke patient?','Immersion in cool water up to the neck','Wrapping in blankets','Giving fluids by mouth','Fanning only','Rapid cooling.'),q('Where are ice packs placed for heat stroke?','Wrists, ankles, armpits and groin','On the forehead only','On the feet only','Nowhere','Large blood vessels.'),q('Hypothermia is body temperature below:','95 °F (35 °C)','99 °F','100.4 °F','90 °F only','Shivering may stop.'),q('Frostbitten skin should not be:','Rubbed','Covered with dry gauze','Rewarmed in warm water when safe','Protected from refreezing','Rubbing damages tissue.'),q('Which is correct for a snakebite?','Keep the person still with the limb at heart level','Cut and suck the wound','Apply ice','Apply a tourniquet','Call 911.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare heat exhaustion and heat stroke, and describe the care for each following HOSA Skill VIII.',rubric:[['Heat exhaustion signs',['sweating','cool','clammy','pale','headache','nausea','dizziness']],['Heat stroke signs',['high','temperature','hot','confusion','seizure','mental status','unconscious']],['Heat exhaustion care',['cool area','back','legs','12 inches','loosen','damp cloths','sips','4 oz','15 minutes']],['Heat stroke care',['911','remove clothing','immerse','neck','sponge','ice packs','wrists','ankles','armpits','groin','shock']]],model:'Heat exhaustion results from losing fluid and salt; the person sweats heavily and has cool, pale, clammy skin, headache, nausea, dizziness, weakness and a fast, weak pulse, but they can still think clearly and their temperature is normal or only slightly raised. Heat stroke is a life-threatening failure of the body’s cooling system: the temperature is very high, often 104 °F or more, the skin is hot and may be red and dry or moist, and the key difference is altered mental status—confusion, slurred speech, seizures or unresponsiveness. For heat exhaustion, after PPE and consent, I move the person to a cool area, have them lie on their back with their legs raised about 12 inches, remove or loosen tight clothing, apply cool, damp cloths and, if they are conscious and can swallow, give small sips of cool water or a sports drink—about 4 ounces every 15 minutes—calling 911 if they vomit or get worse. For heat stroke, I call 911 immediately and cool the person as fast as possible: I move them to a cool area, remove clothing and immerse them in cool water up to the neck, or, if that is not possible, sponge them with cool water and place ice packs at the wrists, ankles, armpits and groin while fanning. I give no fluids if they are confused, watch for shock and am ready to start CPR.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HEAT,CDC_COLD,ARC_STEPS]};

export const F10={title:'Sudden illness, anaphylaxis and opioid overdose (Skills IX and X)',hook:'Some of the most dramatic saves in first aid come from medications a bystander can give in seconds. An epinephrine auto-injector can reverse a severe allergic reaction, and naloxone nasal spray can restore breathing in an opioid overdose. Skills IX and X test both, while the written test covers heart attack, stroke, seizures, diabetic emergencies and poisoning.',objectives:['Recognize and give first aid for heart attack, stroke, seizures, diabetic emergencies and poisoning.','Recognize anaphylaxis and give an epinephrine auto-injector following HOSA Skill IX.','Recognize opioid overdose and give naloxone nasal spray following HOSA Skill X.','Use the recovery position and continue care until EMS arrives.'],sections:[
['Heart attack and stroke',[
'A **heart attack** occurs when blood flow to part of the heart muscle is blocked. Signs include **chest pain, pressure or squeezing** that may spread to the arms, neck, jaw or back, **shortness of breath**, sweating, nausea and lightheadedness; women, older adults and people with diabetes may have **atypical** signs such as fatigue or indigestion. Call 911, have the person rest in a comfortable position and, if they are not allergic and have no bleeding risks, help them chew an **aspirin** (per local protocol). Be ready for CPR and an AED—a heart attack can become cardiac arrest.',
'A **stroke** blocks or ruptures a blood vessel in the brain. Use **BE FAST**: **Balance** loss, **Eyes** (vision loss), **Face** drooping, **Arm** weakness, **Speech** difficulty, **Time** to call 911. **Note the time** symptoms started, because clot-busting treatment is time-sensitive. Do not give food, drink or aspirin to a stroke patient.',
flip('BE FAST',[['B','Balance—sudden loss of balance'],['E','Eyes—sudden vision loss'],['F','Face—drooping on one side'],['A','Arm—weakness or drift'],['S','Speech—slurred or strange'],['T','Time—call 911 and note the time']])]],
['Seizures, diabetic emergencies and poisoning',[
'**Seizures:** keep the person safe—move hard objects away, cushion the head, loosen clothing around the neck, **do not restrain** them and **put nothing in the mouth**. Time the seizure. Afterward, roll them onto their side (**recovery position**) and let them rest. Call 911 if it lasts **more than 5 minutes**, repeats, occurs in water, the person is injured, pregnant or diabetic, has trouble breathing or it is a first seizure.',
'**Diabetic emergencies:** **low blood sugar (hypoglycemia)** develops quickly—shakiness, sweating, hunger, confusion, irritability. If the person is **awake and able to swallow**, give **15–20 grams of fast-acting sugar** (glucose tablets, juice, regular soda) and recheck in about 15 minutes; call 911 if they do not improve or cannot swallow. **High blood sugar (hyperglycemia)** develops slowly with thirst, frequent urination and fruity breath. When unsure, give sugar to a conscious person. **Poisoning:** call **Poison Help (1-800-222-1222)** or 911 if the person is unresponsive or struggling to breathe; do not induce vomiting unless instructed.',
{t:'w',kind:'glucose'},
match('Emergency and first aid',[['Seizure','Protect from injury; nothing in the mouth'],['Seizure lasting over 5 minutes','Call 911'],['Conscious hypoglycemia','15–20 g of fast-acting sugar'],['Unresponsive diabetic','Call 911; nothing by mouth'],['Swallowed poison, awake','Call Poison Help'],['After a seizure','Recovery position']])]],
['Anaphylaxis and the epinephrine auto-injector: Skill IX',[
'**Anaphylaxis** is a severe, whole-body allergic reaction to foods (peanuts, tree nuts, shellfish), insect stings, medications or latex. Signs include **hives**, **swelling of the face, lips, tongue or throat**, **wheezing or trouble breathing**, tightness in the throat, vomiting, dizziness and signs of shock. **Epinephrine is the first-line treatment**; antihistamines are too slow. Call 911 even if symptoms improve, because a **second (biphasic) reaction** can occur.',
'**Skill IX:** **1–3.** Scene safety, ARC or AHA, PPE, introduction and consent, and recognition of the signs. **4.** **Verify** the auto-injector is the patient’s prescribed medication and check the **expiration date**; read the instructions for the **hold time**. **5.** Grasp the device in a **fist** with the tip pointing down. **6.** **Remove the safety cap**. **7.** **Hold the patient’s leg** firmly. **8.** Press the tip **hard against the outer thigh, halfway between the hip and knee**—it can go through clothing—until it clicks. **9.** **Hold** for the manufacturer’s recommended time. **10.** **Pull straight out**. **11.** **Rub** the injection site for about **10 seconds**. **12.** **Note the time**. **13.** Call EMS and **give the used device to EMS**. A second dose may be given in about 5–15 minutes if symptoms persist and a second device is available.',
order('Epinephrine auto-injector',['Verify medication and expiration date; read hold time','Grasp in a fist, tip down','Remove the safety cap','Hold the leg firmly','Press hard on the outer thigh, halfway between hip and knee','Hold for the recommended time','Pull straight out and rub 10 seconds','Note the time; give the device to EMS'])]],
['Opioid overdose and naloxone: Skill X',[
'**Opioids** (heroin, fentanyl, oxycodone, morphine) slow and stop breathing. Signs listed on the rating sheet include **altered level of consciousness**, **choking or gurgling sounds**, **pinpoint pupils** and **blue lips or fingernails**; breathing may be very slow or absent. **Naloxone** quickly reverses opioid effects; it is safe and has no effect on people who have not taken opioids. Many states allow anyone to carry it, and it is sold over the counter.',
'**Skill X:** after scene safety, PPE, consent and recognizing the signs: **verify** the medication name and **expiration date**; lay the person on their back; hold the nasal spray with your **thumb on the plunger** and your **first and middle fingers on either side of the nozzle**; **insert the tip into a nostril until your fingers touch the bottom of the nose**; **press the plunger firmly** to give the dose. Call 911. **If there is no response in 2–3 minutes, a second dose may be given** in the other nostril. **Reassess** breathing; give rescue breaths or CPR as needed, and place a breathing person in the recovery position. Report the **time** of each dose to EMS. Naloxone wears off in 30–90 minutes, so overdose can return.',
order('Naloxone nasal spray',['Recognize overdose signs','Verify name and expiration date','Thumb on the plunger, two fingers by the nozzle','Insert until fingers touch the nose','Press the plunger firmly','Call 911; second dose in 2–3 minutes if no response','Reassess breathing; rescue breaths or CPR; report times'])]],
['The recovery position and ongoing care',[
'A person who is **unresponsive but breathing normally** and has no suspected spinal injury can be placed in the **recovery position**—on their side with the top leg bent and the head supported—so fluids drain from the mouth and the airway stays open. Monitor breathing continuously and be ready to roll them onto their back for CPR.',
'Until EMS arrives, keep the patient comfortable and warm, reassure them and record what happened, signs observed, care given and times. When EMS arrives, give a brief report and hand over medication devices. Then remove gloves properly, dispose of PPE in a biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.',
kase('Restaurant reaction',{a:{text:'A diner with a peanut allergy has hives, a swollen tongue and wheezing; her auto-injector is in her purse. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do first?',choices:[['Verify the medication and expiration date, then inject the outer thigh','b','Correct. Epinephrine first.',true],['Wait to see if she gets better','b','Anaphylaxis can kill quickly.',false]]},b:{text:'You injected at 7:42 p.m. What else?',choices:[['Rub the site 10 seconds, note the time, call 911 and give the device to EMS','c','Correct.',true],['Throw the device in the trash','c','EMS needs it.',false]]},c:{text:'Her breathing improves. You monitor her and watch for a second reaction until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Heart attack: chest pain or pressure—call 911, rest, aspirin if appropriate, be ready for CPR; stroke: BE FAST and note the time; seizures: protect, nothing in the mouth, 911 if over 5 minutes; conscious hypoglycemia: 15–20 g sugar.','Skill IX: verify medication and expiration, fist grip, remove the cap, hold the leg, press hard on the outer thigh halfway between hip and knee, hold, pull out, rub 10 seconds, note the time, give the device to EMS; call 911 for every anaphylaxis.','Skill X: overdose signs are altered consciousness, gurgling, pinpoint pupils, blue lips; thumb on the plunger, insert until fingers touch the nose, press firmly; second dose if no response in 2–3 minutes; reassess, rescue breaths or CPR, report times.'],terms:[['Anaphylaxis','A severe, life-threatening allergic reaction.'],['Naloxone','A medication that reverses opioid overdose.'],['Recovery position','A side-lying position that keeps the airway open.']],mcqs:[q('Where is an epinephrine auto-injector given?','Outer thigh, halfway between hip and knee','Upper arm','Abdomen','Buttock only','It can go through clothing.'),q('After injecting epinephrine, the site is rubbed for about:','10 seconds','2 minutes','1 second','It is never rubbed','Then note the time.'),q('Which is a sign of opioid overdose?','Pinpoint pupils','Dilated pupils','Hyperactivity','Fast breathing','Plus blue lips and gurgling.'),q('How is naloxone nasal spray given?','Insert until fingers touch the nose and press the plunger firmly','Spray into the mouth','Inject into the arm','Squeeze slowly into both eyes','Thumb on the plunger.'),q('When may a second naloxone dose be given?','If there is no response in 2–3 minutes','Never','After 2 hours','Only in the hospital','Use the other nostril.'),q('What does the T in BE FAST mean?','Time to call 911 and note the time','Temperature','Tongue','Tremor','Time-sensitive treatment.'),q('What should you do for a person having a seizure?','Protect them from injury and put nothing in the mouth','Hold them down','Put a spoon in the mouth','Pour water on them','Time it.'),q('What do you give a conscious person with low blood sugar?','15–20 grams of fast-acting sugar','Insulin','Nothing','A large meal of protein','Recheck in 15 minutes.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to recognize and treat anaphylaxis with an epinephrine auto-injector (HOSA Skill IX) and opioid overdose with naloxone nasal spray (HOSA Skill X).',rubric:[['Recognize anaphylaxis',['hives','swelling','tongue','throat','wheezing','breathing','shock']],['Auto-injector',['verify','expiration','fist','cap','outer thigh','hip','knee','hold','rub','10 seconds','time','ems']],['Recognize overdose',['altered','consciousness','gurgling','pinpoint','pupils','blue','lips']],['Naloxone',['thumb','plunger','nozzle','nostril','fingers touch','press','second dose','2-3 minutes','reassess','rescue breaths','cpr']]],model:'Anaphylaxis is a severe allergic reaction that causes hives, swelling of the face, lips, tongue or throat, wheezing or trouble breathing and signs of shock after exposure to a trigger such as peanuts or a bee sting. After scene safety, PPE and consent, I verify that the auto-injector is the patient’s prescribed medication and that it has not expired, and I read the hold time. I grasp it in a fist with the tip down, remove the safety cap, hold the patient’s leg firmly and press the tip hard against the outer thigh halfway between the hip and knee until it clicks, holding it for the recommended time. I pull it straight out, rub the site for 10 seconds, note the time and call 911, giving the used device to EMS because a second reaction can occur. Opioid overdose causes altered consciousness, choking or gurgling sounds, pinpoint pupils, blue lips or fingernails and slow or absent breathing. I verify the naloxone name and expiration date, lay the person on their back, hold the device with my thumb on the plunger and two fingers beside the nozzle, insert the tip into a nostril until my fingers touch the bottom of the nose and press the plunger firmly. I call 911, reassess breathing and give rescue breaths or CPR as needed; if there is no response in 2–3 minutes, I give a second dose in the other nostril and report the times of both doses to EMS.'},sources:[RULES,AAAAI_ANA,SAMHSA_NAL,STROKE]};
